# Batch vs streaming

<PageMeta level="beginner" time="7 min" prereq={[['What is a stream?', '/docs/flink/foundations/what-is-a-stream']]} />

<Objectives>

- Explain why "batch is a bounded stream" is a design claim, not a slogan
- Say what actually changes in the runtime between the two modes
- Decide, for a given requirement, whether streaming is worth the complexity

</Objectives>

## The usual framing, and why it is wrong

People describe these as two philosophies:

> Batch processes data at rest. Streaming processes data in motion.

That is true and not very useful. Here is the framing that changes how you build
things:

<Callout type="key">

**Batch is streaming over a stream that happens to end.**

A nightly job over yesterday's file is the same computation as a streaming job —
you just happen to know where the data stops, so you can be lazier about time,
lateness, and failure.

</Callout>

Flink takes this literally. The same `DataStream` program runs in either mode:

```java
// identical business logic; only the execution mode differs
env.setRuntimeMode(RuntimeExecutionMode.STREAMING);  // unbounded source
env.setRuntimeMode(RuntimeExecutionMode.BATCH);      // bounded source
```

## What actually changes underneath

This is the part worth memorising, because it explains a lot of otherwise
mysterious behaviour.

| | `STREAMING` | `BATCH` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Shuffles** | Pipelined — downstream starts before upstream finishes | Blocking — a stage completes, spills to disk, then the next starts |
| **State** | Kept live, checkpointed | Kept per key, discarded when the key is done |
| **Checkpointing** | On, periodic | Off — recovery is "re-run the failed stage from its spilled output" |
| **Watermarks** | Real, continuously advancing | One watermark at `+∞` at the end |
| **Result emission** | Continuous, possibly updated | Once, at the end |
| **Ordering into operators** | Whatever arrives | Sorted by key, so all records for a key arrive together |
| **Failure cost** | Restore from last checkpoint | Re-run one stage |

That "sorted by key" row is why batch mode needs far less memory: it processes
one key completely, emits, drops the state, moves on. Streaming mode cannot do
that, because a key is never "done".

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph B["BATCH — stages run to completion"]
      direction LR
      B1[Stage 1] -->|spill to disk| B2[Stage 2] -->|spill| B3[Stage 3]
    end
    subgraph S["STREAMING — everything runs at once"]
      direction LR
      S1[Source] --> S2[Operator] --> S3[Sink]
      S3 -.->|backpressure| S2 -.->|backpressure| S1
    end
```

## Real-world analogy

**Batch** is doing your laundry: gather everything, run the machine, get clean
clothes. Efficient per item, but nothing is clean until the whole load finishes.

**Streaming** is a dishwasher plumbed directly to the sink that washes each plate
as you hand it over. Higher machinery cost, more things that can go wrong, but
you never wait for a load.

The dishwasher also has a problem the laundry does not: it must decide what to do
when you hand it a plate from *yesterday's* dinner. That is lateness, and it is
the tax you pay for continuous results.

## When is streaming actually worth it?

Streaming is strictly more machinery. Use it when the answer's *value decays with
age*.

| Requirement | Verdict |
| --- | --- |
| Fraud decision before the payment completes | Streaming. A nightly answer is worthless. |
| Alert when a sensor exceeds threshold | Streaming. |
| Feed a live dashboard | Streaming, usually. |
| Keep a search index fresh | Streaming. |
| Land raw events into a lake for analysts | Streaming *ingestion*, batch analysis. This is what [StreamForge](/docs/intro) does. |
| Monthly financial close | Batch. Correctness beats latency; the data is not final anyway. |
| Retrain a model on two years of history | Batch. |
| Backfill a new metric over historical data | Batch mode of the *same* streaming job — this is the big win of a unified API. |

<Callout type="prod" title="The backfill argument">

The strongest practical reason to use one engine for both: when you add a new
metric, you want to compute it over the last 18 months *and* going forward, and
you want both numbers to agree.

With separate batch and streaming systems you write the logic twice, in two
languages, and the numbers never quite match. This mismatch — reconciling a batch
"truth" table with a streaming "fast" table — is the maintenance cost that made
the **Lambda architecture** fall out of favour.

With one engine you write it once and run it in two modes. That is the **Kappa**
argument, and it is the main reason Flink's unified API exists.

</Callout>

## Micro-batching: the third thing

You will hear "but Spark Structured Streaming does streaming too". It does, via a
different mechanism worth understanding.

```text
Micro-batch:   [ 200ms of data ] → job → [ 200ms of data ] → job → …
True streaming: event → operator → event → operator → …
```

Micro-batching runs a tiny batch job every N milliseconds. That is a legitimate
design with real advantages (simple recovery, high throughput per core). The
trade-offs:

- **Latency floor** — you cannot go below the batch interval, plus scheduling overhead
- **Per-event state and timers are awkward** — "alert if no heartbeat for 30 seconds, per device" fits an event-at-a-time model far more naturally
- **Windows must align to batch boundaries** or be reconciled across them

Flink is event-at-a-time: a record enters an operator, is processed, and leaves.
That is why sub-100ms end-to-end latency and per-key timers are natural in Flink
and fiddly elsewhere.

<Callout type="hood">

"Event-at-a-time" does not mean "one record per network packet". Flink buffers
records into network buffers for throughput and flushes them on a timer
(`execution.buffer-timeout`, default 100ms). Set it to 0 for lowest latency at
the cost of throughput; raise it for the opposite. The *processing model* is
still one record at a time — only the *transport* is batched.

</Callout>

<Callout type="mistake">

Choosing streaming because it sounds more advanced. Streaming adds state
management, watermarks, checkpoint tuning, savepoint-based upgrades, and a class
of bugs that only appear under load.

If a 15-minute-old answer is fine, a cron job and a SQL query will outlive your
streaming pipeline and page you less. Adopt streaming when latency is a
*requirement*, not an aesthetic.

</Callout>

<Callout type="remember">

Batch is a bounded stream. Streaming costs more machinery and buys you answers
whose value would otherwise decay. Pay it only when the decay is real.

</Callout>

